{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"uncheered-sombre-bumblebee","creation_channel":"cli","description":"rebar's philosophy is three interfaces over shared functionality: CLI, MCP, and library. The bridge\nredesign must land on all three, not just the CLI.\n\nToday's MCP and library surfaces are shaped around the retiring vocabulary: `reconcile(mode=...)`\ntakes a mode STRING as user-supplied input (`_mcp_reads.py:291` gates mutating modes on\n`MODE_CAPS[parsed] != 0` behind `mcp.allow_jira_sync`), and `rebar.reconcile(mode=\"dry-run\")` is a\ndocumented library entry point with its own default. Retiring `--mode` changes the MCP tool's input\nschema and the library signature.\n\n`bridge_fsck()` is already exposed as both `rebar.bridge_fsck()` and an MCP tool with a published\n`outputSchema`; `bridge_status` is neither, so the redesign is free to choose its surface there.\n\nNote the library has its own divergent exit policy that must be fixed in the same pass:\n`_lib_ops.py:115` raises unless the code is in `(0, 75)`, so a library caller hitting the normal\nin-flight case (3) gets a `RebarError` while CI treats it as benign. And `_lib_ops.py:92` defaults\n`mode=\"dry-run\"`, a third safety default that disappears with the mode vocabulary.\n\nSince `reconcile` retires with no alias, the MCP tool and library function retire with it — the\nsame reasoning applies: the defaults invert, so a silent rename would change behaviour.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every bridge verb with a machine-consumable result exists on CLI, MCP, and library\n- [ ] The MCP tools' input schemas carry no mode vocabulary\n- [ ] `rebar.reconcile()` is retired alongside the CLI verb; the library gains the bridge equivalents\n- [ ] `_lib_ops.py`'s divergent exit policy is removed\n- [ ] MCP output schemas are published for the new verbs and covered by the existing schema-coverage test\n- [ ] `mcp.allow_jira_sync` still gates exactly the mutating surface","id":"f789-3f9d-8b35-4e0d","parent_id":"2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3","priority":2,"tags":["bridge"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Land the bridge surface on MCP and the library, not just the CLI"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786047587448170001,"uuid":"7f64b981-a398-43b7-9f4b-ab1184665215"}